\paragraph{Software.}
Key library versions: PyTorch 2.x, Transformers 4.x, PEFT 0.x, scikit-learn 1.x.
Exact versions will be pinned in the released code repository.

\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}
LoRA hyperparameters ($r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, dropout $0.05$, lr $2{\times}10^{-4}$, batch size 8, 3 epochs) follow \citet{hu2022lora}.
The Random Forest classifier uses 200 trees, Gini criterion, and no max depth, without hyperparameter search; all reported results use 5-fold stratified cross-validation.

\paragraph{Inference cost.}
Feature extraction and classification require no GPU.
Inference takes approximately 2 minutes per 5{,}000 samples on a single CPU core (reference-model forward pass + RF prediction), enabling deployment without specialized hardware.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.8--85.9\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates bagging (RF) and gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) on the same 19 features; per-cell spread ($\leq$0.4pp) establishes a feature-determined ceiling.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match RF within 0.6\,pp ($p>0.05$); all feature-based methods outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%) and logistic regression (75.0\%).
Binary detectors repurposed for ordinal classification (Binoculars + Fast-DetectGPT combined: 74.2\%) fall 4\,pp below RF (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2, 36.2\%; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 30.4\%) also fail (43\,pp below RF; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}); depth signal therefore resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.2pp due to matched-fold evaluation. 5-fold cross-validation standard deviation $\leq$ 0.9\,pp across all settings.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence confirms the signal resides in the features; three alternative classifiers match RF within 0.8\,pp under cross-model transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}).
Perplexity features alone recover 66.0\% ($-$0.5\,pp vs.\ full), while repetition/diversity reach only 53.0\% ($-$13.6\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:feature_ablation}); perplexity degradation is the dominant depth marker.
Raw perplexity alone performs at chance (25.1\%); the strongest single feature (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) reaches 58.3\% ($-$8.3\,pp vs.\ full); multi-dimensional characterization is required.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread to d1--d3 under transfer (seed std $= 0.06$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:rf_seed}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.9\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline (d1--d3 balanced mean 55.4\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy} reports leave-one-strategy-out transfer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ inverts the d1/d2 boundary under temperature (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + depth classification) recovers 11.5\,pp over naive transfer (Limitations).
A multi-temperature ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}) confirms within-$\tau$ signal for $\tau \leq 0.9$ but collapse at $\tau{=}1.1$; end-to-end PLMs lag RF by 20pp (Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}).

\paragraph{Prompted generation.}
Replicating the GPT-2~XL nucleus pipeline with topic-seeded generation yields 69.5\% (95\% CI $[68.9, 70.2]$, $-$13.8\,pp vs.\ BOS-seeded, $2.8\times$ chance), with monotonic AUC decay and identical top features (Appendix~\ref{app:prompted_gen}): prompt conditioning attenuates but does not eliminate the depth signal.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

Extending to 7--8B under QLoRA, Llama-3.1-8B Instruct \cite{llama3} achieves 70.2\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}4$) and Qwen2.5-7B \cite{qwen2.5} 60.1\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{>}5$).
A base-model control (Llama-3.1-8B, 59.9\%, non-monotonic AUC) vs.\ instruction-tuned (70.2\%, monotonic) suggests instruction tuning \emph{regularizes} recursive degradation; a 1.5B instruct control (47.4\%, $1.9\times$ chance) confirms at matched scale (Appendix~\ref{app:instruct_1p5b}; confounds in Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_confounds}).
Cross-model chains (84.75\%), cross-domain (arXiv 74.1\%), and LoRA rank sensitivity ($\Delta{=}4.65$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}) further confirm robustness.

\section{Additional Results}
\label{app:additional}

\subsection{Top-$k$ Feature Trajectories}
\label{app:topk_trajectories}

Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory} in the main text shows feature trajectories for nucleus ($p{=}0.95$) and temperature ($\tau{=}0.9$) sampling.
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) trajectories follow the same monotonically decreasing trend for all features but with compressed inter-depth spacing, consistent with the signal attenuation effect discussed in \S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}.
Concretely, the d0$\to$d1 drop in \texttt{p90\_ppl} under top-$k$ is 78\% (vs.\ 87\% for nucleus), and the d2$\to$d3 reduction is 31\% (vs.\ 39\% for nucleus).
Despite the magnitude reduction, feature rank ordering across depths is preserved, which explains why ordinal classifiers maintain directionality even under top-$k$ conditions.

\subsection{Permutation Importance}
\label{app:permutation}

To verify that the Gini-based importance rankings in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} are not an artifact of the impurity measure, we compute permutation importance (5-fold CV, 10 repeats) for all 9 atlas cells.
The top-2 features are stable across measures: \texttt{p90\_ppl} ranks in the top 2 in 9/9 cells under both Gini and permutation importance, and \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9 (vs.\ 7/9 for Gini, with the additional cell being OLMo top-$k$; the single SHAP exception is GPT-2~XL top-$k$, where \texttt{p75\_ppl} displaces \texttt{var\_surprisal} to rank 3).
Kendall's $W{=}0.90$ across all three methods (Gini, permutation, SHAP) indicates strong concordance ($p{<}0.005$).
Pairwise Spearman correlations are: SHAP--Gini $\rho{=}0.93$, SHAP--permutation $\rho{=}0.84$, Gini--permutation $\rho{=}0.76$.
Per-cell top-5 overlap between SHAP and permutation importance is 3--4 out of 5 features; the aggregated (mean-across-cells) top-5 sets are identical; the top features are therefore robust to the choice of importance measure at the aggregate level, though per-cell rankings exhibit model-dependent variation.

\subsection{Per-Class Recall}
\label{app:per_class}

Table~\ref{tab:per_class_recall} reports per-class recall across all 9 atlas cells (4-class in-distribution, 5-fold CV).
Depth~0 recall exceeds 98\% uniformly.
Among generated depths, d2 is the hardest class in every cell; the deficit is most pronounced under top-$k$ sampling (46--53\%), where vocabulary truncation removes the tail tokens that carry depth-discriminative signal.

